00:03.960 --> 00:07.880
Okay, so what is exactly top or in other databases we call it limits.

00:07.880 --> 00:10.320
So it is again some kind of filtering in SQL.

00:10.360 --> 00:15.480
If you use it it's going to go and restrict the number of rows returned in the results.

00:15.480 --> 00:18.800
So you have a control on how many rows you want to see in the results.

00:18.800 --> 00:20.640
The syntax is very simple as well.

00:20.640 --> 00:26.480
Directly after the selects you can use the keyword top, and then you specify the number of rows you

00:26.480 --> 00:27.680
want to see in the results.

00:27.680 --> 00:28.880
So for example three.

00:28.920 --> 00:31.960
And then only after that you specify the columns that you want.

00:32.000 --> 00:33.200
And then from which table.

00:33.240 --> 00:35.040
Now let's see how SQL can execute it.

00:35.040 --> 00:37.240
So as usual the from can be executed.

00:37.240 --> 00:38.440
We will get our data.

00:38.440 --> 00:41.440
And then the second step is going to go and select the columns.

00:41.480 --> 00:43.200
In this case all the columns can stay.

00:43.240 --> 00:45.840
And then after that it's going to execute that top.

00:45.840 --> 00:47.600
So how it works it's very simple.

00:47.600 --> 00:50.720
For each row in database we have row number.

00:50.720 --> 00:55.560
It has nothing to do with your data with the IDs for example here like in the current result we have

00:55.600 --> 00:57.840
row number 12345.

00:57.880 --> 01:00.400
Those numbers are not your actual data.

01:00.400 --> 01:02.440
It is something technical from the database.

01:02.440 --> 01:04.360
So it is not equal to the IDs.

01:04.360 --> 01:07.760
For example, the IDs is actually your content, your data.

01:07.760 --> 01:11.880
So here we are not filtering based on the data based on the row numbers.

01:11.880 --> 01:14.840
So since here we have defined three SQL going to count okay.

01:14.880 --> 01:16.480
Row number 123.

01:16.520 --> 01:17.360
And that's it.

01:17.360 --> 01:18.720
So it can make a cut.

01:18.720 --> 01:23.200
And all the rows after number three they will be excluded from the results.

01:23.200 --> 01:25.840
And you will get only the three rows at the results.

01:25.840 --> 01:30.320
So now as you can see this type of filtering is not based on a condition or something.

01:30.320 --> 01:32.440
It's just based on the row numbers.

01:32.440 --> 01:37.160
So whatever results you have in your data, it will go and make a cut at a specific row.

01:37.160 --> 01:39.880
